:root{color-scheme:light}body{margin:0;background:#efe8d9;color:#29251e;font:18px/1.7 Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif}#ui-bar{display:none}#story,#ui-bar.stowed~#story{margin:0!important}#passages{max-width:760px;margin:auto;padding:0 24px 48px}.passage{background:#fffcf5;padding:28px 36px;border-radius:0 0 20px 20px;min-height:75vh;box-shadow:0 12px 40px #30251612;overflow:auto}h1,h2{line-height:1.15;letter-spacing:-.025em}h1{font-size:2.2rem;margin:16px 0 20px}h2{font-size:1.6rem}h1:focus{outline:none}p{margin:0 0 1.15em}.eyebrow,.speaker,.stone-stats,button,summary,.charge{font-family:system-ui,sans-serif}.eyebrow{font-size:.72rem;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.17em;color:#68604f}.scene-banner{height:150px;background:#95836b url('village.webp') center 58%/cover;margin:-28px -36px 24px}.speaker{font-size:.8rem;color:#71502c;font-weight:650}.portrait{float:right;width:150px;height:180px;object-fit:contain;object-position:bottom;margin:0 -10px 15px 20px}.stone-choice,button{display:block;width:100%;margin:12px 0;padding:15px 18px;text-align:left;border:1px solid #bcae8e;border-radius:10px;background:#f4efdf;color:#263e3b;font-size:1rem;line-height:1.5;cursor:pointer;min-height:48px}.stone-choice:hover,button:hover{background:#e3eadf;border-color:#577a6d}button:focus-visible,a:focus-visible,summary:focus-visible{outline:3px solid #197e8b;outline-offset:3px}button:disabled{color:#746f62;background:#eeeae2;cursor:not-allowed;opacity:.75}.stone-stats{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);gap:8px;margin:18px 0 8px}.stone-stats>div{padding:9px 12px;background:#eee9dc;border-radius:8px}.stone-stats span{display:block;font-size:.7rem;color:#656056}.stone-stats strong{display:block;font-size:.82rem;font-weight:600}details{font-size:.88rem;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd2ba;padding:12px 0;margin-bottom:15px}summary{cursor:pointer;color:#38665f;font-weight:600}details p{margin:12px 0 0}.charge{font-size:.72rem;color:#49796d;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.1em}.score{padding:16px;background:#e4eee2;font-size:1.3rem;border-radius:10px}a{color:#22645b}.passage li{margin:8px 0}.stone-note{padding:12px 16px;border-left:3px solid #bcb08d;font-size:.85rem;background:#f5f0e6}@media(max-width:520px){body{font-size:17px}#passages{padding:0 0 24px}.passage{padding:22px 20px;border-radius:0;box-shadow:none}h1{font-size:1.85rem}.scene-banner{margin:-22px -20px 20px;height:125px}.portrait{width:110px;height:150px;margin:0 -2px 10px 12px}.stone-choice{font-size:.95rem}}@media(prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){*{animation:none!important;transition:none!important;scroll-behavior:auto!important}}


:: Start
<div class="scene-banner"></div><div class="eyebrow">The Teaching Stone · Episode 01</div><h1>The Grain We Keep</h1><p>A new body. A borrowed seal. A village that needs more than good intentions.</p><p>Ten decisions · About 10–15 minutes · Read at your own pace</p><p>Open this episode from your Ethics and Leadership class to save progress. This is historical fiction; the characters, village and stone are invented.</p><<if window.parent === window>><<button "Read a standalone preview">><<run setup.ready=true; setup.refresh()>><</button>><<else>><p>Connecting to your class…</p><</if>>

:: Decision1
<div class="scene-banner"></div><div class="eyebrow">Episode 01 · Decision 1 of 10</div><h1>Whose voice comes first?</h1><<stonePortrait "scribe">><p class="speaker">Djehu · the scribe</p><p>The bus horn is the last sound you remember. You pushed a stranger out of its path. A flash of white. Then heat, mudbrick, and a hand shaking your shoulder.</p><p>Your hands are older. Your clothes are linen. Somehow the words around you make sense. ‘Administrator? They have been waiting since dawn.’ Djehu presses a seal into your palm. Outside, a line of people bends around the grain store. The seal gives you <strong>authority</strong>: a recognized right to decide. It does not prove your decisions will be just.</p><p>You are responsible for this estate’s village. The river rose poorly; a canal bank has collapsed. Your store holds 60 measures of food grain and 20 of seed grain. These are simplified game units, not a reconstruction of ancient accounting. You cannot buy your way out with money you do not have.</p><p>Under the seal lies a smooth dark stone. A pale line flickers inside it. Djehu does not seem to notice. ‘The landholder expects to be heard first,’ he says. Behind him, a woman holds an empty basket.</p><details><summary>Ask Djehu what the seal means</summary><p>‘It makes your orders official. It does not make them wise.’ He taps his board. ‘My duty is to record what leaves this store. Yours is harder: deciding whose needs the record should notice. If you make me hide the shortage, I can keep a tidy account of a dying village.’ You remember a question from your old classroom: who gets to decide what is right? Here, unsettlingly, the answer is you.</p></details><<stoneStats>><div class="charge">The stone · 0% charged</div><<stoneChoice "listen" "Hear the households and workers before making promises.">>
<<stoneChoice "ledger" "Inspect the stores and ask who is missing from the account.">>
<<stoneChoice "command" "Receive the landholder first; authority needs influential friends.">>

:: Consequence1
<div class="eyebrow">The village answers</div><h1>After your decision</h1><<stonePortrait "scribe">><<stoneConsequence 0>><<stoneStats>><<button "Continue" "Decision2">><</button>>

:: Decision2
<div class="scene-banner"></div><div class="eyebrow">Episode 01 · Decision 2 of 10</div><h1>The first bread</h1><<stonePortrait "household">><p class="speaker">Tia · household representative</p><p>‘My neighbor has three children,’ Tia says. ‘My brother has two strong sons who can work. If you give each house the same amount, it will look fair on the board. It will not last the same number of days.’ Djehu says equal shares are the <strong>custom</strong>—the established way of doing things. Tia asks whether a familiar rule still fits this year’s needs.</p><p>Djehu suggests a public feast: it would restore confidence. Hori wants food held for the people repairing the canal. Tia notices your own untouched breakfast. ‘You should eat too. But please do not make my children wait until you understand everything.’</p><p>A needs-based ration costs 16 measures. Equal household shares cost 18. A feast costs 36. There is no outside delivery you can simply assume will arrive. Your first order will teach the village what kind of fairness to expect.</p><details><summary>Ask Tia about people who cannot queue</summary><p>‘A widow on the east lane cannot walk here. Two families work fields beyond the canal. They never heard there would be a hearing. Let us carry their shares, and let someone check that we did.’ Considering everyone requires more than listening to whoever is loudest or nearest. Her request has <strong>reasons</strong> behind it: different needs and barriers to access, not simply a wish for special treatment.</p></details><<stoneStats>><div class="charge">The stone · 10% charged</div><<stoneChoice "needs" "Distribute by need, with public reasons and delivery to absent households (16 grain).">>
<<stoneChoice "equal" "Give every household an equal share (18 grain).">>
<<stoneChoice "feast" "Hold a generous feast to end today’s hunger and fear (36 grain).">>
<<stoneChoice "reserve" "Keep the store closed until the influential families agree.">>

:: Consequence2
<div class="eyebrow">The village answers</div><h1>After your decision</h1><<stonePortrait "household">><<stoneConsequence 1>><<stoneStats>><<button "Continue" "Decision3">><</button>>

:: Decision3
<div class="scene-banner"></div><div class="eyebrow">Episode 01 · Decision 3 of 10</div><h1>Who repairs the water?</h1><<stonePortrait "worker">><p class="speaker">Hori · irrigation worker</p><p>Hori spreads reeds across the ground to show the broken canal. ‘Repair the bank properly and the next water will reach the lower fields. Patch the top and it will look sound from the road. Until it breaks.’</p><p>The workers need rations. They also have families and fields of their own. Intef says you can require service. Hori replies, ‘You can command our hands. Hunger will still decide how long they work.’</p><p>A paid crew costs 12 grain. A shorter shared-work schedule costs 6 and requires some of your own time. Forced labor saves grain but places the burden on people with little power to refuse. Delaying leaves the lower fields exposed. These are <strong>consequences</strong>: what your decision makes happen, including costs that appear after the applause.</p><details><summary>Ask Hori why he doubts the quick repair</summary><p>‘My father patched this bank twice. Each time the water cut underneath it. We need to strengthen the base.’ He is not asking you to ignore the account. He is asking you to count knowledge that never appears on it.</p></details><<stoneStats>><div class="charge">The stone · 20% charged</div><<stoneChoice "paid" "Pay a crew and use Hori’s deeper repair plan (12 grain).">>
<<stoneChoice "shared" "Agree on shorter shifts and share the work yourself (6 grain).">>
<<stoneChoice "forced" "Require unpaid work; the canal serves everyone.">>
<<stoneChoice "delay" "Delay repairs and preserve today’s supplies.">>

:: Consequence3
<div class="eyebrow">The village answers</div><h1>After your decision</h1><<stonePortrait "worker">><<stoneConsequence 2>><<stoneStats>><<button "Continue" "Decision4">><</button>>

:: Decision4
<div class="scene-banner"></div><div class="eyebrow">Episode 01 · Decision 4 of 10</div><h1>A gift with a shadow</h1><<stonePortrait "landholder">><p class="speaker">Intef · landholder</p><p>Intef arrives without guards. He carries a small loaf. ‘My mother remembered a year like this,’ he says. ‘I would rather not bury people. I also cannot promise every sack my estate owns.’</p><p>He offers 24 grain if hungry families promise future labor until their debt is repaid. There is no fixed end date. He calls this security. Tia calls it a door that locks from the outside.</p><p>You could negotiate 16 grain in exchange for publicly recorded recognition, with no claim on household labor. Or require a 12-grain contribution from his surplus and publish the rule. His support matters. So do the lives he wants as collateral.</p><details><summary>Ask Intef what he fears losing</summary><p>‘If I give once, every shortage becomes mine to solve. If I refuse, everyone remembers only my full store.’ You can understand his fear without accepting his terms. Public recognition might motivate a real contribution. Mixed motives do not make every benefit false. The stone offers a question from your old life: would you accept these terms if you woke tomorrow in the body of the person taking the loan?</p></details><<stoneStats>><div class="charge">The stone · 30% charged</div><<stoneChoice "bargain" "Negotiate a fixed contribution and public credit, without labor debt (+16 grain).">>
<<stoneChoice "debt" "Accept the larger loan secured by household labor (+24 grain).">>
<<stoneChoice "levy" "Require a proportionate surplus contribution with a published reason (+12 grain).">>
<<stoneChoice "refuse" "Refuse his help rather than owe him influence.">>

:: Consequence4
<div class="eyebrow">The village answers</div><h1>After your decision</h1><<stonePortrait "landholder">><<stoneConsequence 3>><<stoneStats>><<button "Continue" "Decision5">><</button>>

:: Decision5
<div class="scene-banner"></div><div class="eyebrow">Episode 01 · Decision 5 of 10</div><h1>The missing measure</h1><<stonePortrait "scribe">><p class="speaker">Djehu · the scribe</p><p>At sunset, Djehu discovers a measure missing. Someone names a young worker. A guard is already leading him forward. ‘Make an example,’ a voice says. ‘Otherwise tomorrow everyone will steal.’</p><p>Hori says the worker carried a ration to an injured friend. Tia says she saw a basket spill near the door. Djehu’s board contains a corrected mark. None of those facts tells the whole story.</p><p>The worker’s hands shake. You could punish him, hide the discrepancy, forgive him immediately, or stop to compare the record with witnesses. Keeping order is part of your duty. So is asking what kind of order you are keeping. <strong>Impartiality</strong> means judging without unfairly favoring either side—even when one person is powerful or one story is satisfying.</p><details><summary>Read the corrected entry</summary><p>The original mark counted a delivery twice. That may explain part of the shortage, but it does not prove everything the worker says. A fair inquiry needs both the corrected account and the people who saw the delivery. Evidence can complicate a satisfying story. Independent support for a claim is <strong>corroboration</strong>. Three people repeating one rumor are still relying on one source.</p></details><<stoneStats>><div class="charge">The stone · 40% charged</div><<stoneChoice "investigate" "Hear witnesses and correct the account before assigning responsibility.">>
<<stoneChoice "punish" "Punish the accused worker to discourage future theft.">>
<<stoneChoice "conceal" "Quietly change the record so the village appears well managed.">>
<<stoneChoice "mercy" "Release the worker without investigating; compassion matters most.">>

:: Consequence5
<div class="eyebrow">The village answers</div><h1>After your decision</h1><<stonePortrait "scribe">><<stoneConsequence 4>><<stoneStats>><<button "Continue" "Decision6">><</button>>

:: Decision6
<div class="scene-banner"></div><div class="eyebrow">Episode 01 · Decision 6 of 10</div><h1>What does order require?</h1><<stonePortrait "scribe">><p class="speaker">Djehu · the scribe</p><p>Djehu stays after the others leave. ‘You keep asking what makes an order fair. We speak of ma’at: truth, justice, and the proper order of the world. But powerful people can say “order” when they mean “leave my place untouched.”’</p><p>The stone in your palm warms. A sentence appears like light beneath water: A village can be quiet because it trusts its leaders—or because it fears them.</p><p>Djehu asks what principle you want written beside your seal. Your old classroom’s four lenses return: <strong>authority</strong>, <strong>custom</strong>, <strong>reasons</strong>, and <strong>consequences</strong>. They can work together. They can also expose what another lens hides. These are your modern tools for examining this fictional situation, not a claim that every ancient Egyptian used this framework.</p><details><summary>Open your four-lens notebook</summary><p>Authority: who has a recognized right to decide? Custom: what established practice are people relying on? Reasons: what supports a decision, and would the same reasoning apply to a powerful friend? Consequences: who benefits, who suffers, and what happens later? Ma’at joins truth, justice, and proper order in an Egyptian religious world. Dignity calls for respectful treatment. Duty asks what you owe because of your role or relationship. Use these ideas together; the oldest rule or strongest official does not automatically settle the question.</p></details><<stoneStats>><div class="charge">The stone · 50% charged</div><<stoneChoice "balance" "Use my authority to test custom, give fair reasons, and check consequences.">>
<<stoneChoice "obedience" "Authority and custom settle it: people should obey the administrator.">>
<<stoneChoice "kindness" "My generous intentions are enough; consequences cannot make the decision wrong.">>

:: Consequence6
<div class="eyebrow">The village answers</div><h1>After your decision</h1><<stonePortrait "scribe">><<stoneConsequence 5>><<stoneStats>><<button "Continue" "Decision7">><</button>>

:: Decision7
<div class="scene-banner"></div><div class="eyebrow">Episode 01 · Decision 7 of 10</div><h1>Tomorrow’s bread</h1><<stonePortrait "household">><p class="speaker">Tia · household representative</p><p>Tia returns with a child asleep against her shoulder. You can see now how easily the word “household” hides a person. She does not ask for a speech about the future. She asks whether there will be bread tomorrow.</p><p>There are 20 measures of seed grain set aside. Eating seed can relieve an emergency. It also means planting less. Hori says the repaired bank—if you funded it—makes protecting seed especially valuable. If the bank remains weak, seed alone is no guarantee.</p><p>You can protect all the seed, release five measures as a limited emergency ration, or use fifteen. None of these choices creates new grain. Your earlier decisions determine how desperate this moment has become. Your role gives you a <strong>duty</strong> to respond. It does not give you the ability to guarantee everything.</p><details><summary>Ask what Tia would count as a promise</summary><p>‘Do not promise that no one will ever be hungry. Promise to tell us what remains, who receives it, and when you will reconsider. A promise I can question is worth more than a beautiful promise I cannot check.’ <strong>Dignity</strong> is the worth that calls for respectful treatment. It does not depend on whether the person asking is convenient, grateful, or powerful.</p></details><<stoneStats>><div class="charge">The stone · 60% charged</div><<stoneChoice "preserve" "Protect all seed and explain the remaining food ration.">>
<<stoneChoice "ration" "Release five seed measures for urgent needs; protect the other fifteen.">>
<<stoneChoice "consume" "Release fifteen seed measures to relieve more hunger now.">>

:: Consequence7
<div class="eyebrow">The village answers</div><h1>After your decision</h1><<stonePortrait "household">><<stoneConsequence 6>><<stoneStats>><<button "Continue" "Decision8">><</button>>

:: Decision8
<div class="scene-banner"></div><div class="eyebrow">Episode 01 · Decision 8 of 10</div><h1>The administrator is a person too</h1><<stonePortrait "worker">><p class="speaker">Hori · irrigation worker</p><p>You wake before dawn with a headache. Every dispute now arrives at your door. A leaking jar. A missing tool. A cousin who wants a larger share. You remember a different life, school corridors, the bus, a person you hope survived.</p><p>Hori finds you asleep over the accounts. ‘If you must approve every repair, the canal will wait whenever you close your eyes.’ Djehu proposes a small team with clear responsibilities and an open record. Intef offers to take all the decisions off your hands.</p><p>Rest can look selfish when others are struggling. But exhaustion can also turn your generosity into a system nobody else can operate.</p><details><summary>Ask Hori about sharing responsibility</summary><p>‘Give us authority to fix small problems and a rule for reporting what we use. Let Tia check who receives food. Let Djehu keep the account. Then someone can catch my mistakes as well as yours.’ Trust needs both room to act and ways to answer for actions.</p></details><<stoneStats>><div class="charge">The stone · 70% charged</div><<stoneChoice "delegate" "Share defined responsibilities and rest, while keeping decisions open to review.">>
<<stoneChoice "alone" "Keep every decision yourself and work through the night.">>
<<stoneChoice "elite" "Hand control to Intef; he has experience and resources.">>

:: Consequence8
<div class="eyebrow">The village answers</div><h1>After your decision</h1><<stonePortrait "worker">><<stoneConsequence 7>><<stoneStats>><<button "Continue" "Decision9">><</button>>

:: Decision9
<div class="scene-banner"></div><div class="eyebrow">Episode 01 · Decision 9 of 10</div><h1>Whose name remains?</h1><<stonePortrait "landholder">><p class="speaker">Intef · landholder</p><p>Intef points to the granary wall. ‘You could put your name there. People remember a name longer than a ration.’ You feel the attraction. In this borrowed life, perhaps you could leave proof you mattered.</p><p>Tia wants the account displayed: what came in, what went out, and who can challenge it. Hori wants the workers named. Djehu says there is room for a record of contributions, but a grand monument will cost eight measures of grain.</p><p>Wanting to be remembered is not automatically corrupt. A useful legacy can begin in pride. The question is what your desire for recognition asks other people to pay. <strong>Accountability</strong> means being answerable for what you do. An impressive name on a wall can invite questions—or distract from them.</p><details><summary>Remember your agreement with Intef</summary><p>If you negotiated grain for public credit, honest acknowledgment keeps your promise. Credit need not mean unlimited praise or authority. If you required a contribution, record it accurately too. Public truth should not depend on whether you like the contributor.</p></details><<stoneStats>><div class="charge">The stone · 80% charged</div><<stoneChoice "publish" "Display the accounts, promises, and all actual contributions—including yours.">>
<<stoneChoice "monument" "Spend eight grain on a monument celebrating your leadership.">>
<<stoneChoice "credit" "Honor the workers and contributors, with a modest record of your own role.">>

:: Consequence9
<div class="eyebrow">The village answers</div><h1>After your decision</h1><<stonePortrait "landholder">><<stoneConsequence 8>><<stoneStats>><<button "Continue" "Decision10">><</button>>

:: Decision10
<div class="scene-banner"></div><div class="eyebrow">Episode 01 · Decision 10 of 10</div><h1>A village beyond you</h1><<stonePortrait "household">><p class="speaker">Tia · household representative</p><p>The pale line inside your stone is now a complete circle. You know, without being told, that this life is nearly finished. The village does not know. For them, tomorrow is not an epilogue.</p><p>Tia lays a hand on the granary door. ‘A good administrator can die. A bad one can inherit the seal. What have we got then?’</p><p>You can leave a council that includes households and workers, or train a successor with public records and a way to appeal decisions. Either can fail; either gives the village something beyond your intentions. You could instead trust your reputation, or spend one final generous ration and let tomorrow’s leader worry about tomorrow.</p><details><summary>Ask who can safely disagree</summary><p>Tia answers immediately: ‘The person with the least to lose can always speak. I want the person with the most to lose to be able to speak too.’ A council needs more than seats. A successor needs more than your confidence. The agreement must name how complaints are heard and how decisions can be corrected.</p></details><<stoneStats>><div class="charge">The stone · 90% charged</div><<stoneChoice "council" "Leave a representative council, public accounts, and a protected route for complaints.">>
<<stoneChoice "successor" "Train a successor and establish public reviews and appeals.">>
<<stoneChoice "secrecy" "Leave the seal to a loyal ally and keep the accounts private.">>
<<stoneChoice "empty" "Give a final generous ration; leave the next crisis to the next leader (12 grain).">>

